# Rust Style

Applies to every Rust file in this repo from the moment one lands (a future
native shell or crate). The naming bans in `typescript-style.mdc` apply to
Rust in full (no `state`/`data`/`type`/`manager`/`handler`/`utils`/`fns` in
names we own; "Protocol" as a suffix is a manager-word: name the owned
thing). The comment rules in `documentation-comments.mdc` apply unchanged,
including the copy-paste litmus.

## Errors: typed at the core, serialized at the boundary (HARD RULE)

`Result<T, String>` is banned in code we own. A predecessor app accumulated
162 stringly-typed Results and 192 `map_err(|e| format!(...))` translations,
and the UI could never tell retryable from fatal. The shape that replaces
it:

- **Internal errors are `thiserror` enums** owned by the domain module that
  produces them (`#[derive(Debug, thiserror::Error)]`, `#[from]` for
  source-error conversion). One enum per domain, not one giant app enum.
- **Errors crossing IPC are wire shapes**: a serializable struct/enum with
  a `kind` discriminator the TS side can branch on, plus a human message.
  Commands return `Result<T, WireXError>`, converted from the internal enum
  via `From`. Tauri requires `Serialize` on command errors anyway; use that
  to ship structure, never `.to_string()`.
- **Bag-of-context errors** (`anyhow`-style) are allowed only in flows
  where no caller will ever branch on the failure, and never cross IPC, a
  crate boundary, or a thread boundary that the UI observes. When in
  doubt, type it. (Community consensus is intent-based, not
  library-vs-application: type the errors callers react to.)
- The map_err ladder is a smell: if a function body translates more than
  one error with `map_err`, give the module its own error enum with
  `#[from]` conversions and let `?` do the work.

## No swallowed failures (`no-swallowed-errors.mdc`, Rust face)

- `let _ = fallible()` is the Rust spelling of an empty catch. Either
  handle the `Result`, propagate it, or surface it through the logging
  path. The one legal discard is an expected, named condition with a
  comment naming why it is normal.
- Event emits, cleanup, and shutdown paths surface their failures through
  `tracing` (`warn!`/`error!` with the subsystem field), not `eprintln!`
  and not silence. `print_stdout`/`print_stderr` are deny lints; logging
  goes through the tracing pipeline once it exists.
- Panics are not error handling. `unwrap_used` and `expect_used` are deny
  lints. The one sanctioned `expect` is the outermost process entry
  (`run()`), carrying `#[expect(clippy::expect_used, reason = ...)]`.

## Lints are law

The workspace lint table in the root `Cargo.toml` is the single source:
clippy `all` + `pedantic` as warn, with the panic/print/wildcard families
at deny. Rules of engagement:

- Never weaken the workspace table to silence a finding. Escape hatches are
  per-site `#[expect(lint, reason = "...")]` (which errors when it stops
  firing), never `#[allow]` and never file-wide.
- New warnings are fixed in the change that introduces them. `cargo clippy
  --all-targets` runs clean before any Rust change ships.
- A pedantic member that proves to be pure churn across several real hits
  may be moved to `allow` in the workspace table with a one-line comment
  (`missing_errors_doc`/`missing_panics_doc`/`module_name_repetitions` are
  the usual candidates). That is a deliberate table edit, not a local
  override.

## Module shape

- `lib.rs` stays a thin registry: module declarations, command
  registration, service construction in `setup`. Command functions are
  one-to-three-line delegations into domain modules. A predecessor's
  961-line lib.rs and 2,292-line catch-all module are the anti-pattern.
- A domain module owns its commands, its wire shapes, and its internal
  error enum. Orchestrators read as numbered stages; source quirks and
  retries live in named step files (`code-organization.mdc` applies to
  Rust modules the same as TS).
- Wire shapes carry the `Wire` prefix on both sides of the boundary and
  are updated with their TS mirror in the same change (until tauri-specta
  or equivalent generates the TS side).
- Long-lived services are constructed once in `setup` and reached through
  `tauri::State`. Name the thing itself (`Nas`, `CloudApi`, `UploadQueue`),
  never `*Manager`/`*Protocol`/`*Handler`/`*Service`.

## Async and blocking

- Commands that do I/O are `async fn` and run on the async runtime.
- CPU-heavy or blocking-disk work goes through `spawn_blocking` (or a
  bounded worker), never inline in an async command and never on the main
  thread. Bound parallelism explicitly (semaphore/const), never unbounded
  spawns.
- Main-thread-pinned platform APIs (Core Location and friends) get their
  pinning documented at the construction site, naming the constraint.

## Dependencies and workspace hygiene

- One Cargo workspace at the repo root: shared `[workspace.package]`
  (edition, rust-version), `[workspace.dependencies]` as the single
  version source, members opting in via `workspace = true`.
- Every dependency earns its place: no dep lands before the code that uses
  it, `default-features = false` plus named features where the crate is
  heavy (the reqwest treatment), and the supply-chain gate applies to
  crates.io exactly as it does to npm: prefer versions that have soaked.
- `rust-version` is a contract: bump it deliberately, in its own change,
  never as a side effect.

## Idiom floor (edition 2024, rustc 1.96)

- `let Some(x) = ... else { return ... }` over nested `if let` pyramids.
- `?` everywhere errors propagate; no manual match-and-return ladders.
- Derive over hand-written impls; `#[serde(rename_all = "camelCase")]` on
  every wire shape so the TS side never sees snake_case.
- Iterators over index loops when the loop body reads better; index loops
  when they don't. Slices (`&[T]`) over `&Vec<T>` in signatures.
- Borrow first: `&str` over `String` parameters unless ownership is the
  point. Clones are deliberate and rare, not reflexive.
- Tagged enums over parallel booleans, exactly like the TS rule.

## Tests

- Unit tests live beside the code in `#[cfg(test)] mod` blocks; the
  testing discipline in `testing.mdc` (what earns a test, failure-mode
  matrices) applies unchanged.
- Keep commands thin so domain logic tests never need a runtime shell.
- Snapshot tests earn their place for parser/extractor code with real
  fixture files.
